in this lesson we're going to talk about  translating sentences into equations so  some key words that you want to be  familiar with and understand are these  ones here so let's start off at the top  so when you see more than  that means add  when you see less than that means  subtract but what sometimes students get  a little confused by is when you say you  know  the number is  2 less than x what students will do is  they'll say 2 minus x because they're  trying to translate like in a direct way  they're saying 2 less than  x but that's actually not correct what  you want is you want x and 2 less than  that means you're taking 2 away from x  okay so don't do a direct translation  when you're  using the phrase you're less than  okay and then product means multiplier  times quotient means divide and another  thing that students and i wonder about  when you're doing the quotient is you  know which one is divided by which one  well if they say the quotient of uh  five quotient of 5 and 10  you take the first number that goes in  the numerator and the second number that  goes in the denominator  okay and then sum means add difference  means subtract and then if you see the  word is that means equals and of  means times so review these and memorize  those that will help you let's go into a  couple examples go ahead and pause the  video  see if you can do these on your own and  we'll go through them together so the  first one says  the product of a number and 6  is 2 less than the quotient of 105.  what is the number  okay so let's translate this into an  equation and we'll solve it so the  product of a number in 6. we don't know  what the number is so let's just call  that n for number okay the product means  we're multiplying so product of n and 6  is means equals  2 less than the quotient of 105. so the  quotient of 105 means 100 divided by 5  2 less than that means we're subtracting  2. okay so we've set up our equation now  let's just solve it so 100 divided by 5  is 20.  okay  n times 6 is just 6 times n or 6n  20 minus 2 is 18.  divide both sides by 6 and you can see  that n equals 3  and you got it  okay let's go to number 2 here jane is 2  more than 3 times her daughter's age if  her daughter is 12 how old is jane  okay so let's see if we can write this  equation here so jane we'll just say j  for jane  is okay equals  two more than so two plus  three times her daughter's age so let's  just say her daughter's age in this case  they say it's 12 so we'll put 12 in for  her daughter's age right  okay so all we have to do is simplify  this one pretty easy we've got 2 plus 3  times 12 is 36  so j equals 2 plus 36 which is 38 and  you got it so again knowing how to  translate sentences into equations using  these terms and phrases will make it a  lot easier when you see these problems  on the act
 

